{"key":"t65-bio-1.1","kind":"mcq","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"A","final_answer":"Much of the heat added to water is used to break hydrogen bonds between water molecules before the molecules can move faster.","working":"Specific heat is the amount of heat that must be absorbed or lost for 1 g of a substance to change its temperature by 1°C. Temperature measures the average kinetic energy (motion) of molecules. Because water molecules are extensively hydrogen-bonded to one another, much of the added thermal energy is initially absorbed to break these intermolecular hydrogen bonds rather than immediately increasing the kinetic energy of the molecules. Consequently, water requires a relatively large amount of heat energy to increase its temperature compared to other substances of similar molecular mass.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":412,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":412,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":448,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":160,"reasoningTokens":288},"totalTokens":860,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":288,"promptTokenCount":412,"candidatesTokenCount":160,"totalTokenCount":860,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":412}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":160}]},"reasoningTokens":288,"cachedInputTokens":0},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":4914,"keyed_label":"A"}
