{"key":"t65-bio-x4","kind":"mcq","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"A","final_answer":"Much of the heat added to water is used to break hydrogen bonds between water molecules before the molecules can move faster.","working":"Water's high specific heat means it absorbs considerable heat with only a small temperature increase. Temperature measures average molecular kinetic energy. In liquid water, added heat first disrupts extensive hydrogen bonds between molecules rather than immediately increasing molecular motion, so much energy goes into breaking these intermolecular attractions. Choices B, C, and D are incorrect: covalent bonds do not break during ordinary heating, evaporation is not the main reason for specific heat, and density does not trap heat in the way described.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":466,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":82,"cacheReadTokens":384},"outputTokens":265,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":148,"reasoningTokens":117},"totalTokens":731,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":466,"completion_tokens":265,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":384,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":82,"total_tokens":731,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":384},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":117}},"reasoningTokens":117,"cachedInputTokens":384},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":5306,"keyed_label":"A"}
{"key":"t65-bio-x4","kind":"mcq","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"A","final_answer":"Much of the heat added to water is used to break hydrogen bonds between water molecules before the molecules can move faster.","working":"Specific heat is the amount of heat energy required to raise the temperature of 1 gram of a substance by 1°C. Temperature measures the average kinetic energy (speed of movement) of molecules. In liquid water, molecules are held together by an extensive network of hydrogen bonds. When heat is added, a significant portion of the thermal energy is initially absorbed to disrupt these intermolecular hydrogen bonds rather than immediately increasing the kinetic energy (motion) of the molecules. Consequently, water can absorb a large amount of heat with only a relatively small change in temperature, giving it a high specific heat.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":412,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":412,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":454,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":173,"reasoningTokens":281},"totalTokens":866,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":281,"promptTokenCount":412,"candidatesTokenCount":173,"totalTokenCount":866,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":412}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":173}]},"reasoningTokens":281,"cachedInputTokens":0},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":4400,"keyed_label":"A"}
